<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wetterau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wetterau is a fertile lowland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogelsberg">Vogelsberg</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, named after the river Wetter. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> enclosed it within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes Germanicus</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a land of imperial cities such as Friedberg and of comital houses such as Isenburg, Hanau and Solms, which formed the Wetterau Association of counts. It remains an important farming region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wetterau.jpg" alt="Wetterau" width="320" height="85" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MdE · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wetterau_W-E.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
